      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hazelwood was settled in the late 18th century and named after John Welch’s Hazelwood estate, famous for its hazelnut trees.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trawellino.com/destinations/united-states/north-carolina_hazelwood"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Shelton House museum in Hazelwood preserves the region’s heritage and showcases artifacts from the town’s early days.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trawellino.com/destinations/united-states/north-carolina_hazelwood"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hazelwood was officially incorporated as a town by the North Carolina General Assembly in 1905.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazelwood%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1995, Hazelwood merged with the neighboring town of Waynesville after facing financial challenges.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazelwood%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today Hazelwood is a neighborhood of Waynesville, reflecting its long history as a former incorporated town.",
